<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Bonnie Blue Flag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a> (first sung in Jackson, Mississippi, spring <a href="/years/1861">1861</a>; published in New Orleans, <a href="/years/1861">1861</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonnie-blue-flag">Bonnie Blue Flag</a>&quot;, also known as &quot;We Are a Band of Brothers&quot;, is a marching song of 1861 associated with the Confederate States of America. The entertainer Harry McCarthy wrote the words and set them to the melody of &quot;The Irish Jaunting Car&quot;. The title refers to the Bonnie Blue Flag, the unofficial first flag of the Confederacy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Harry McCarthy first sang the song at a concert in Jackson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, and performed it again in September 1861 at the New Orleans Academy of Music for the First Texas Volunteer Infantry regiment as it mustered in. The New Orleans music publishing house of A. E. Blackmar issued six editions between 1861 and 1864, with three further arrangements. When Major General Benjamin Butler captured New Orleans, he is said to have arrested Blackmar, fined him $500, destroyed the copies of the music and ordered a fine of $25 for anyone caught singing or whistling it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first line, &quot;We are a band of brothers, and native to the soil&quot;, recalls the St Crispin's Day speech in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>'s Henry V. The Library of Congress copy of 1861 continues &quot;Fighting for our liberty with treasure, blood, and toil&quot;, while another 1861 version has &quot;fighting for the property we gained by honest toil&quot;. Eleven further editions were published with different words, and the Confederate widow Annie Chambers Ketchum published new verses as &quot;The Gathering Song&quot;.</p><p>The full song names the seceding states verse by verse, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, ending with the single star of the flag grown to eleven. For the sake of the metre the third verse changes the order of secession: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> is said to take South Carolina by the hand, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> left the Union before it. Another verse hails Davis as &quot;our loved President&quot; and Stephens as a &quot;statesman rare&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>As with many songs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, the tune also served the Union. J. L. Geddes, a British-born colonel, wrote &quot;The Bonnie Flag With the Stripes and Stars&quot; in 1863, singing of the Union and equal rights, and a Mrs C. Sterett published another Union version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Irish Volunteers&quot;, sung by the 69th New York regiment of the Irish Brigade, used the same melody.</p><p>The song has been heard in many films, among them Gone with the Wind of 1939, where Rhett Butler names his daughter Bonnie Blue Butler, The Searchers, The Horse Soldiers, The Good, the Bad and the Ugly, Glory and Gettysburg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Bonnie Blue Flag” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
